The fear that free money causes laziness contradicts what we observe: lottery winners show minimal lasting changes in work behaviour; inheritance recipients in most studies continue working.
Entrepreneurship increases when people have a financial safety net — UBI functions as startup capital for small businesses that are currently never started because the founder cannot absorb a period of zero income.
The gig economy's growth means more workers lack employer-provided benefits; UBI provides portable social protection that follows workers rather than being tied to a specific employer.
The Mincome experiment in Dauphin, Manitoba (1970s) showed the only groups that worked less were new mothers and teenagers — both of whom society broadly agrees should be doing exactly that.
Universal programmes have higher political durability than means-tested ones because everyone benefits — Social Security in the US has survived many attempts to cut it precisely because it is universal.
